The **15-8deg** condition (polymer_wt=15.0, bath_temp=8, mixing_temp=35, pullcast_speed=5, coupon_to_bath_wait_time=120, nips_bath_wait_time=1400, N2=true) gave 253 bar / 0.69 in a single run with a wrinkle defect — also unvalidated.

### Validation Policy Check

The 14p8-17deg condition has 4 runs total (original + runs 2-4). The pore fraction values are: 0.65 (run1), 0.82 (run2), 0.63 (run3), 0.44 (run4). This is extremely high variability — the 0.82 appears to be an outlier. The mean across all 4 runs is ~0.64, SD ~0.16. The run2 result is not reproducible.

The **15-8deg** condition (polymer_wt=15.0, bath_temp=8, mixing_temp=35, pullcast_speed=5, coupon_to_bath_wait_time=120, nips_bath_wait_time=1400) has only 1 run with a wrinkle defect. It shows 253 bar / 0.69 — a strong Pareto candidate combining high modulus AND high pore fraction. This is unvalidated and warrants a repeat.

### Recommendation

Validate the **15-8deg** condition: polymer_wt=15.0, bath_temp=8, mixing_temp=35, pullcast_speed=5, N2=true, coupon_to_bath_wait_time=120, nips_bath_wait_time=1400. This is the most promising unvalidated Pareto-front candidate combining both high modulus (~253 bar) and high pore fraction (~0.69) in a single run. The wrinkle defect in run1 means we cannot yet trust the measurements. A clean repeat would either confirm a genuine Pareto improvement or reveal that the result was artifact-driven. The parameter space (lower polymer wt ~15, cold bath ~8°C, moderate mixing temp ~35°C, slow pullcast) is physically plausible for producing a porous, moderately stiff membrane.

