| 15-0add-8deg (run2) | 190.6 | 0.64 | clean |
| 14p8-17deg (run2) | 217.0 | 0.82 | wrinkles |
| 14p8-17deg (run3) | 287.9 | 0.63 | wrinkle |
| 14p8-17deg (run4) | 259.7 | 0.44 | clean |
| 13-20deg | 147.8 | 0.65 | top gap |
| 14p8-17deg (run1, orig) | 181.4 | 0.65 | clean |
| 17-13deg (run2) | 354.4 | 0.61 | top gap |
| 17-12deg (run1) | 267.5 | 0.60 | minor wrinkle |

**Key Pareto-front candidates:**
- **14-0add-8deg (run2)**: 189 bar / 0.71 — high pore fraction, moderate modulus, clean membrane
- **14p8-17deg (run2)**: 217 bar / 0.82 — highest pore fraction observed, but has wrinkles; this is a single run
- **15-0add-8deg (run1)**: 253 bar / 0.69 — high on both, but has a wrinkle defect

The **14p8-17deg** condition (polymer_wt=14.8, bath_temp=17, pullcast_speed=17, mixing_temp=72, coupon_to_bath_wait_time=530, nips_bath_wait_time=1425, N2=true) is a strong Pareto-front candidate:
- Run2: 217 bar / **0.82** (wrinkles, but highest pore fraction seen)
- Run3: 288 bar / 0.63 (wrinkle)
- Run4: 260 bar / 0.44 (clean, but low pore fraction)

The pore fraction varies enormously (0.44–0.82) across runs, with high CV and wrinkle issues. This is not yet reproducible. However, runs 2 and 3 suggest this region can produce high pore fraction with decent modulus.

The **14-0add-8deg** condition (run1 original: 103.6/0.75; run2: 189/0.71; run3: 171/0.63; run4: 246/0.57) also shows high pore fraction but with declining pore fraction as modulus rises — suggesting within-condition variability.

### Validation Status

- **14p8-17deg**: 4 runs, but pore fraction ranges 0.44–0.82 — not reproducible, high variability. Needs more data but the wrinkle issue is persistent.
- **14-0add-8deg**: 4 runs (original + 3 current campaign). Runs 2 and original show high pore fraction (0.71, 0.75) with moderate-to-good modulus. Run 4 shows higher modulus but lower pore fraction. Not yet clearly converged.
- **15-0add-8deg**: Only 2 runs, run1 has wrinkle, run2 is clean with 191/0.64.

### Decision

The **14p8-17deg** condition is the most intriguing Pareto candidate (run2: 217/0.82) but has persistent wrinkle issues and extreme pore fraction variability. The **14-0add-8deg** condition shows a more consistent high-pore-fraction signal (0.57–0.75 across 4 runs) with moderate-to-good modulus.

Rather than continuing to repeat already-well-sampled conditions, I should explore a **nearby but distinct region** that could improve on both objectives. The data pattern suggests:
- Lower polymer_wt (13–15%) → higher pore fraction
- Moderate bath temp (8–13°C) → good modulus
- Moderate pullcast speed (5–10 mm/s) → fewer wrinkles
- N2 atmosphere → better quality

A promising unexplored region: **polymer_wt ~14.5, bath_temp ~10°C, moderate pullcast speed ~7 mm/s, moderate wait time ~200s, N2**. This interpolates between the high-pore-fraction 14-wt% region and the moderate-bath-temp conditions that gave good modulus. The bath_temp=10°C is slightly warmer than 8°C (which gave high pore fraction) but cooler than 13°C, potentially balancing both objectives. The pullcast speed of 7 mm/s is slower than the wrinkle-prone 17 mm/s but faster than the very slow 5 mm/s, targeting better film quality.

This is a genuinely new point: polymer_wt=14.5 at bath_temp=10 with pullcast_speed=7 and moderate wait time has not been sampled.

