**Key Pareto-relevant observations:**
- **14p8-17deg run2**: Pore fraction 0.82 is the highest observed, with modulus 217 bar — but it has wrinkles. This is a single measurement with no clean repeat at these conditions.
- **14-0add-8deg run2**: Modulus 189, PF 0.71 — clean membrane, good balance.
- **14p5-10deg**: Modulus 222, PF 0.67 — clean, single measurement.
- **15-0add-8deg run2**: Modulus 191, PF 0.64 — clean.

The 14p8-17deg run2 result (PF=0.82) is striking but came with wrinkles and is a single run. The original 14p8-17deg run1 (PF=0.65, modulus=181, clean) was already validated once. Runs 2–4 show high variability in pore fraction (0.82, 0.63, 0.44), suggesting this condition is unstable.

**14p5-10deg** (polymer_wt=14.5, bath=10°C, mixing=45°C, speed=7, wait=200s, N2, nips=1500s) gave modulus=222, PF=0.67 with a clean membrane — a single measurement that sits well on the Pareto front. This is an under-validated promising candidate.

**14-0add-8deg** (polymer_wt=14.0, bath=8°C, mixing=30°C, speed=5, wait=120s, N2, nips=1250s) has 4 runs with PF ranging 0.57–0.75 and modulus 103–246. The mean PF is high (~0.67) but modulus is variable. Run 2 (PF=0.71, modulus=189) is the best clean result.

### Validation Decision

The **14p5-10deg** condition (polymer_wt=14.5, bath_temp=10, mixing_temp=45, pullcast_speed=7, coupon_to_bath_wait_time=200, nips_bath_wait_time=1500, N2=true) produced modulus=222 bar and PF=0.67 with a clean, defect-free membrane in a single run. This sits on or near the Pareto front (better PF than most high-modulus candidates, better modulus than most high-PF candidates). It has not been independently reproduced. Per the validation policy, this candidate should be repeated before treating it as a confirmed Pareto improvement.

This region (lower polymer wt ~14–15%, moderate bath temp ~8–12°C, moderate mixing temp ~35–50°C, moderate speed ~5–10 mm/s) consistently produces higher pore fractions while maintaining reasonable modulus — a physically plausible pattern where lower polymer concentration and moderate bath temperature slow phase separation enough to develop more open pore structure without collapsing mechanical integrity.

